The RFC 08 §7 decode seam. `schema check --schema-set` is the offline half —
"needs no session at all — that is the whole point" (`cmd/schema.rs`) — and its
three exit codes are pinned in `schema-check.trycmd`.

```console
$ zenctl schema --help
Dump a producer's served payload schemas (`@rpc/<producer>/describe`), or check a payload against one (`schema check`, #159).

RFC 08 §7: the shapes are served data. A producer that serves no `describe` degrades honestly — it is not an error.

Usage: zenctl schema [OPTIONS] [PRODUCER]
       zenctl schema <COMMAND>

Commands:
  check  Validate one payload against its schema — no bus write, exit-coded for CI (#159): 0 = valid, 1 = does not conform, 2 = could not check
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [PRODUCER]
          Producer name, e.g. `sysinfo`

Options:
      --type <TYPE>
          Show only this type (implies the full document)

      --full
          Print every schema document in full, not just kind + hash

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. When given, registry-sourced commands answer offline from these files instead of the live bus

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Seconds to wait for replies / to watch the bus (default 5; a context may override the default)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl schema check --help
Validate one payload against its schema — no bus write, exit-coded for CI (#159): 0 = valid, 1 = does not conform, 2 = could not check.

The schema comes from a live `describe` (give --producer) or an offline SchemaSet JSON document (--schema-set; the registry TOMLs carry type *names*, not shapes, so a directory alone cannot check).

Usage: zenctl schema check [OPTIONS] --type <TYPE> --from <TEXT|@FILE|->

Options:
      --type <TYPE>
          Type name to validate against

      --from <TEXT|@FILE|->
          Payload: inline text, `@file`, or `-` for stdin

      --producer <PRODUCER>
          Producer whose served `describe` carries the schema (live mode)

      --schema-set <FILE>
          SchemaSet JSON document (RFC 08 §7) — offline mode, no session

      --encoding <ENCODING>
          Wire encoding of the payload. Defaults to the schema kind's own (json-schema → JSON, protobuf → protobuf, cdr → XCDR1)

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. When given, registry-sourced commands answer offline from these files instead of the live bus

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Seconds to wait for replies / to watch the bus (default 5; a context may override the default)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

```
